cff-version: 1.2.0
title: pi-canon
message: >-
  If you use this software or its measurements, please cite the paper that
  covers the part you used. The three are listed under references below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Conner
    given-names: Shane
repository-code: https://github.com/shaneconner/pi-canon
url: https://shaneconner.com/projects/pi-canon/
abstract: >-
  Canonical project memory for coding agents: one mutable article per governed
  asset at a knowable address, an append-only journal beneath it, and recall
  that arrives when an asset is touched rather than when the agent thinks to
  ask. Ships for Pi, Codex, and Claude Code.
keywords:
  - agent memory
  - long-term memory
  - project memory
  - append-only journal
  - canonical addressing
  - context engineering
  - coding agents
license: MIT
version: 0.2.4
date-released: "2026-08-22"
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Mutable Canonical Memory over an Immutable Journal, with Recall by
      Surfacing
    authors:
      - family-names: Conner
        given-names: Shane
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21890647
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      The first campaign. Whether the design holds up at all, measured against a
      no-extension floor that received the prior transcripts.
  - type: article
    title: Pricing Recall in Long-Term Memory for AI Agents
    authors:
      - family-names: Conner
        given-names: Shane
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21960350
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      Six studies on what recall costs and which parts earn their keep. Priced
      the orientation line and the tool schema, both negative and both deleted.
  - type: article
    title: "pi-canon: The Write Desk"
    authors:
      - family-names: Conner
        given-names: Shane
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22057257
    year: 2026
    notes: >-
      What gets written down and whether it stays true. Writers left superseded
      values standing in records whose contract is to state what is true now. A
      direction that survived three captures and a magnitude that did not.
